From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 02:42:42 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Spread a match start's chunk work over ticks

The diagnostics kit found a 0.5-1.4 s tick whenever an SG match started (20 bots, Lobby_Games).
JFR showed what it was made of, and each part is now spread out or gone:

- ChunkIOExecutor finished every load the I/O threads had completed in one tick: ~660 chunks'
  entities, ChunkLoadEvent and neighbours (0.55 s) when 20 players arrived in a new world. A tick
  now spends at most chunkLoadFinishMillisPerTick (pandaspigot.yml, default 10) and leaves the rest
  for the next ticks. A load a synchronous get() finishes meanwhile is not finished twice; a world
  unloaded meanwhile does not get its chunk put back (WorldServer.athenaUnloaded).
- A waiting load held its whole NBT, block arrays included: the compound is trimmed to what the
  main-thread half reads once the chunk is built (only for compounds read from disk, not ones taken
  from the save queue). Without this the budget ran a 144M heap into seven full GCs.
- Unloading saves every chunk of an ordinary world, changed or not, ~1 ms each on this thread, 100 a
  tick: an emptied lobby made ~140 ms ticks. chunkUnloadMillisPerTick (default 10) stops earlier.
- Template worlds skip recreateStructures on load: they generate nothing, and the village and
  stronghold checks ran hundreds of random seeds per chunk.
- Chunks loaded by AthenaChunkPreloader get their packet cache built as they load and compressed on
  a low-priority thread, instead of on the first send when every player arrives at once.

sgbench3, 20 bots, longest tick at the match start (with SG loading the arena 10 s ahead):
base 995 / 1,439 ms -> 136 / 129 ms; this server alone with the old SG: 352 ms. Heap after GC peak
101-105 MB -> 56-61 MB. Server tests 5518/5518.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

